From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:55:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 570/570] media: i2c: hi556: write the analogue gain to its own
 register

The analogue gain is a 16-bit write to 0x0077, whose low byte lands in 0x0078, the
Gr digital gain high byte. 0x0077 itself only ever gets the high byte, which is
zero for any value in range. So the control leaves the analogue gain at its minimum
and, while streaming, raises the Gr gain.

The vendor driver for this register map writes 16 bits at 0x0076, and 0x0077 is
the low byte of that pair. Do the same.
---
 drivers/media/i2c/hi556.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/hi556.c b/drivers/media/i2c/hi556.c
index 756f004b4..4f1dbb0c4 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/hi556.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/hi556.c
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@
 #define HI556_EXPOSURE_STEP		1
 
 /* Analog gain controls from sensor */
-#define HI556_REG_ANALOG_GAIN		0x0077
+#define HI556_REG_ANALOG_GAIN		0x0076
 #define HI556_ANAL_GAIN_MIN		0
 #define HI556_ANAL_GAIN_MAX		240
 #define HI556_ANAL_GAIN_STEP		1
@@ -879,7 +879,7 @@ static int hi556_init_controls(struct hi556 *hi556)
 	int ret;
 
 	ctrl_hdlr = &hi556->ctrl_handler;
-	ret = v4l2_ctrl_handler_init(ctrl_hdlr, 8);
+	ret = v4l2_ctrl_handler_init(ctrl_hdlr, 10);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
